  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Homeland Security obligated $57.6B in contracts in FY2026 to date. The Department of Homeland Security recorded 22,141 awards with an average award size of $2.6M. Obligations are up 103.5% year over year. Fiscal-year comparisons show a steady rise from $19.7B in FY21 to $28.3B in FY25, and a sharp increase to $57.6B in FY26. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest NAICS category at $28.5B, followed by Ship Building and Repairing at $7.7B. Contractors focused on construction, shipbuilding, security services, logistics consulting, and IT captured the top shares of spending. FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO is the largest vendor at $13.4B, followed by BARNARD CONSTRUCTION COMPANY, INCORPORATED at $4.5B and DAVIE DEFENSE INC. at $3.5B. This mix shows heavy investment in physical infrastructure and maritime capability alongside ongoing purchases of security services and IT support.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How did DHS contract spending change year over year?",
        "body": "The Department of Homeland Security obligated $57.6B in FY26 to date, up 103.5% year over year. Fiscal-year obligations increased from $19.7B in FY21 to $28.3B in FY25, then rose to $57.6B in FY26. The agency issued 22,141 awards in FY26 to date with an average award size of $2.6M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What are DHS's largest contract categories and who wins them?",
        "body": "Commercial and Institutional Building Construction accounted for $28.5B of obligations, the largest NAICS category. Ship Building and Repairing totaled $7.7B, and Security Guards and Patrol Services totaled $3.4B. FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO received $13.4B, BARNARD CONSTRUCTION COMPANY, INCORPORATED received $4.5B, and DAVIE DEFENSE INC. received $3.5B."
      }
    ]
  },
